{% set local_problem %}
<p>Surface drainage doesn't work on sloped lake lots. Here's why:</p>

<p>Most <a href="/recent-projects/">Guntersville waterfront properties</a> slope toward the lake. That's intentional — you paid for the view. But "sloped toward the lake" means water follows gravity in that direction. Including the water coming off your roof.</p>

<p>A downspout extension that deposits water 6 feet from your foundation doesn't solve the problem. That water just runs 6 feet sideways, then continues downhill toward the lake. If your house is between the roof runoff and the lake, the water goes past your house first. Past your foundation wall. Past your lower-level living space.</p>

<p>We see this constantly on <a href="/recent-projects/">Buck Island</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">Wyeth Drive</a>, the <a href="/recent-projects/">Peninsula properties</a>. Homeowners who've tried splash blocks, extensions, even French drains along the foundation. The water still finds its way to the lower level because all those solutions fight gravity. They delay the problem; they don't solve it.</p>

<p>Underground drains work with gravity instead of against it. Water enters at the downspout, travels through a sealed pipe, and exits at a point far enough past the house that lot grade carries it away from the foundation rather than toward it. On a steep lake lot, that might mean 40-60 feet of pipe to get water truly past the problem zone.</p>

<p>The investment pays for itself. Foundation repair on a lake home runs $5,000-15,000+. Lower level moisture problems cost even more when you factor in mold remediation and finished space damage. Underground drainage that prevents these problems is cheap by comparison.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water to a discharge point away from your foundation. Gravity does all the work.</p>

<p>We use 4-inch schedule 40 PVC for most installations. It handles more flow than any downspout can deliver, won't degrade over time, and routes cleanly around obstacles. Some applications use corrugated pipe for flexibility, but PVC is our preference for permanent installations — smoother interior flows better and resists clogging.</p>

<p>The system runs through a trench 8-12 inches deep, sloped at 1-2% grade (1-2 inches of drop per 8 feet of run). We use solid pipe — not perforated drain tile meant for French drains, which clogs with silt and defeats the purpose.</p>

<p>At the discharge end, options include: pop-up emitters that release water at grade level, daylight exits that open at the lot edge, or direct discharge toward the lake on waterfront properties. We design the exit based on your lot conditions and where water can go without causing new problems.</p>

<p>Critical details: cleanout access at direction changes (lets you clear rare clogs), proper slope throughout (flat sections hold water and eventually cause problems), and sized transitions from downspout to pipe (no pinch points that create bottlenecks).</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed underground drainage throughout the <a href="/recent-projects/">Guntersville lake area</a> for over 20 years. <a href="/recent-projects/">Buck Island</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">Point of Pines</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">North Lake Drive</a>, the steep lots along <a href="/recent-projects/">Wyeth Drive</a> — we know the terrain and we know what works.</p>

<p>Standard residential drainage assumes flat lots. Lake lots aren't flat. We design systems that account for your actual grade, your actual soil conditions, and where water actually needs to go. That's the difference between a solution that works and another thing you tried that didn't.</p>

<p>For vacation homeowners, underground drains are ideal: install once, works forever, zero maintenance. Your property stays protected whether you're there or not.</p>
{% endset %}

